  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7b8860589b05b4642ab42b24201a782d, type: 3}
  m_Name: Book_KeepingHens
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: GrandmasHouse::GrandmasHouse.Data.NoteData
  kind: 3
  title: Keeping Hens
  recipe: {fileID: 0}
  body: 
  marginNote: 
  pages:
  - Four good hens will give you an egg apiece most mornings, if you keep them fed,
    watered and shut in at night. That's all a hen asks of you.
  - 'Feed and water


    A scoop from the feed sack goes a good way in the feeder.
    They''ll eat kitchen scraps too, even ones gone soft, and they go wild for the
    hornworms picked off the tomatoes, so keep a pail by the garden for those.


    Fill
    the waterer from the watering can. A hen with nothing to eat or drink for three
    days won''t see a fourth.'
  - 'Shutting them in


    At dusk the hens walk in on their own if the little door
    is open. Close it behind them. A door left open all night, or a hen left outside
    it, is how you meet the fox.


    Open it again in the morning so they can get
    out to scratch.'
  - 'Eggs, and the pot


    Look in the nest boxes every morning. Eggs keep about
    three weeks and go into the soup pot whole.


    When times are lean a hen will
    feed the family one last time. A chicken only keeps a couple of days, so cook
    it, or smoke it or salt it down.'
  mentions: []
